  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 200 PM CDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nThe lobe of mid-level vorticity that spawned the Colorado low \nmoves into the Upper Mississippi River Valley before being \nejected eastward by the zonal flow. This drags the low pressure \ncenter northeastward towards Iowa. As the winds shift to out of \nthe southeast, a warm, moist air is pulled northward into the \nCWA, setting the stage for another round of heavy rain north of \nI-80 Friday night into Saturday morning. Latest ensemble \nguidance shows a swath of 0.25-0.75 of an inch along the Highway\n20 corridor (50-70% chance). The coverage and intensity of \nthese storms will decrease throughout the day on Saturday but \nremain scattered north of I-80 as the low pressure center \ndrifts into northern Iowa.\n\nSaturday night-Monday...A shortwave trough that has been \ncamping across the western CONUS for most of the week will \nfinally propagate eastward into the Northern Rockies Saturday \nnight. Shortwave ridging over the Mid-to-Upper Mississippi River\nValley introduces southwesterly flow aloft, pulling another \nmid-level vorticity lobe northward into the north-central \nPlains. This mid-level trough is forecasted to redevelop storms \n(50-70% coverage) along a cold front moving through the area. \nThe SPC has a Marginal Risk, Level 1 out of 5, for most of the \noutlook area for isolated damaging winds, but the latest LREF \nguidance only has a 20-40% joint probability of SBCAPE exceeding\n500 J/kg and the sfc-to-500 mb bulk shear exceeding 30 knots. \nTherefore, any convection that forms should struggle to \norganize.\n\nAfter Saturday night, scattered PoPs (40-70%) will stick around\ninto Monday as weak shortwaves rotating around the main trough \naxis, now over the Northern Plains, keeps CVA over the area. By \nMonday though, most precipitation will be in the form of rain \nshowers as the boundary and low pressure over Missouri drifts \nfurther south and northeasterly flow from a high pressure over \nOntario pumps in cooler air.\n\nTuesday-Wednesday...The shortwave trough over the Northern \nPlains finally reaches the Midwest on Tuesday. Isolated cold- \ncore showers could stick around during the day before subsidence\nsets in on the backside of the trough heading into Tuesday \nnight and Wednesday. The persistent northeasterly flow should \nallow for seasonably cool temperatures with highs only in the \nlower to upper 60s!",
